From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nate Isern <nate@oxy.so>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Serve astro://settings from Astro, under upstream's own host

Astro's settings surface is a Vite + Bloom application, not Chromium's Polymer
one. This is the line that makes the browser serve it.

WHY THE REGISTRATION IS SWAPPED AND NOT ADDED

Two independent reasons, and either one alone would force this shape.

The first is the private extension APIs. `settingsPrivate` -- the only
transport that carries a pref's POLICY state (`controlledBy`, `enforcement`,
`userSelectableValues`) alongside its value -- is granted by host pattern, at
chrome/common/extensions/api/_api_features.json, and the pattern names the
settings host. Seven more APIs are granted to that same host across the two
feature files -- autofillPrivate, languageSettingsPrivate, passwordsPrivate,
inputMethodPrivate and quickUnlockPrivate in that one, metricsPrivate and
management in extensions/common/api/_api_features.json -- counted by grepping
both for `chrome://settings/*`. A settings page served under any other host
gets none of the eight, and gets them silently:
the binding is simply never installed, so the first control to read it throws
`Cannot read properties of undefined` on a page that otherwise looks correct.
Serving under the same host inherits every grant with no change to the grant
table at all.

The second is that adding a second config for the same origin is not an
option: `WebUIConfigMap::AddWebUIConfigImpl` CHECKs on a duplicate origin, so
registering Astro's config alongside upstream's would crash the browser at
startup rather than shadow it. An earlier `AstroSettingsUIConfig` did exactly
that and was deleted.

Swapping the line makes both true by construction, and makes the diff the
review: one host, one config, and it is visible here which one.

WHAT UPSTREAM'S SETTINGS LOSES, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT

`settings::SettingsUI` is no longer reachable by navigation, but it is still
compiled and still linked, and so are its 38 message handlers -- Astro's
controller adopts four of them directly (browsing-data deletion, search
engines, about/version, browser lifetime) and will adopt more. This patch
removes a registration, not an implementation.

The include is swapped rather than left in place: with the config gone,
nothing else in this translation unit names `settings::SettingsUI`.

Owned by https://github.com/OxyHQ/Astro/issues/15.
---
diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/webui/chrome_web_ui_configs.cc
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/adblock/webui/astro_adblock_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_alia_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_ntp_ui.h"
+#include "chrome/browser/oxy/webui/astro_settings_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/about/about_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/accessibility/accessibility_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/actor_internals/actor_internals_ui.h"
@@ -112,7 +113,6 @@
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/privacy_sandbox/privacy_sandbox_dialog_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/profile_internals/profile_internals_ui.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/search_engine_choice/search_engine_choice_ui.h"
-#include "chrome/browser/ui/webui/settings/settings_ui.h"
